(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-coronary-artery-disease-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Coronary Artery Disease Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement defines angina pectoris?","choices":["Permanent scarring of the ventricle","Inflammation of the pericardium","Transient ischemia short of infarction","Myocardial necrosis with ST elevation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Angina pectoris is transient myocardial ischemia that is not long or severe enough to cause infarction, and its symptoms are recurrent. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How is non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction treated compared with unstable angina?","choices":["Observation only","The same way","As an ST-elevation infarct","Thrombolytics first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction is treated the same as unstable angina: a medical emergency with chewable aspirin, a beta blocker, intravenous nitrates and antithrombotic therapy, then catheterization. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 48, 49"},{"q":"Which coronary artery supplies the atrioventricular node?","choices":["Left marginal artery","Right coronary artery","Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The right coronary artery supplies the atrioventricular node, which is why right coronary artery occlusion is the one most likely to lead to dysrhythmias such as complete heart block. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"From what age is age itself a coronary risk factor in men?","choices":["65 years","35 years","45 years","55 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age is a nonmodifiable risk factor from 45 years in men and from 55 years in women; men carry more risk than premenopausal women, a gap that narrows with age. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Above which standard troponin I value, in ng/mL, is the result abnormal?","choices":["0.004","0.04","0.4","4.0"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The 99th percentile for the standard troponin I assay is 0.04 ng/mL; a standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL is abnormal, and a result at or below it is reported as not abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"ST elevation in V1 through V4 points to which culprit artery?","choices":["Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Right marginal branch","Left anterior descending"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The anterior and septal leads V1 through V4 point to the left anterior descending artery, now named the anterior interventricular artery, the most common culprit in acute infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"How does the coronary risk gap between men and women change with age?","choices":["It narrows by about 80","It widens every decade","It stays fixed for life","It favors women at 40"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Men carry more risk than premenopausal women, but the difference narrows with age and is close to even by about 80. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which feature marks angina as unstable rather than stable?","choices":["Pain at rest","Pain with exertion","Relief with nitroglycerin","Substernal pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stable angina is relieved by rest; unstable angina occurs at rest. Pain at rest, new-onset angina that limits activity, or angina that is increasing makes the pattern unstable. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 45"},{"q":"On a standard 12-lead electrocardiogram, how does a posterior infarct appear?","choices":["ST depression in V1 to V3","ST elevation in V1 to V3","ST elevation in II, III, aVF","ST depression in V5 and V6"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A posterior infarct shows as ST depression in V1 to V3, the reciprocal of ST elevation on the back of the heart. It is easy to miss, and posterior leads V7 to V9 confirm it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"In roughly what share of right coronary occlusions does the infarct extend into the right ventricle?","choices":["15% to 30%","1% to 5%","50% to 60%","Over 90%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Right coronary occlusion affects the inferior and posterior left ventricular wall and the septum, and extends into the right ventricle in 15% to 30% of cases. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"When is a thrombolytic given for ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["When chest pain has resolved","When intervention is 2+ hours away","When troponin is still normal","For every patient on arrival"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If the closest percutaneous coronary intervention unit is 2 or more hours away, thrombolytics are given, started within 30 minutes of arrival. They carry a risk of intracerebral hemorrhage. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 67, 74"},{"q":"Which agent is delivered at angiography to provoke coronary spasm?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Diltiazem","Amlodipine","Ergonovine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ergonovine or acetylcholine is delivered during angiography; coronary spasm that then resolves with nitroglycerin through the catheter is the gold standard for vasospastic angina. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"When is a stent usually placed during percutaneous coronary intervention?","choices":["Only after a failed atherectomy","Only after bypass grafting","After balloon pre-dilation","Before any guidewire is passed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The stent is usually placed after balloon pre-dilation of the narrowed segment. Direct stenting, without pre-dilation, may lead to better outcomes, so the order is not absolute. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 69, 70"},{"q":"How does body position affect the pain of angina pectoris?","choices":["Leaning forward relieves it","Sitting upright worsens it","It does not change it","Lying flat relieves it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pain of angina pectoris is not affected by position; it typically worsens with exertion. Pain that changes with position lowers the suspicion that it is cardiac. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"When a vein is used for coronary bypass, what does it connect?","choices":["The two ends of the blocked segment only","The aorta and the coronary beyond the block","The coronary artery and the coronary sinus","The pulmonary artery and the coronary artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A vein graft, usually the great saphenous vein, forms a connection between the aorta and the coronary artery distal to the obstruction. It is used when an artery cannot be. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is true of coronary artery distribution from person to person?","choices":["It differs only between sexes","It is fixed by the left main","It varies between people","It is identical in everyone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Coronary distribution varies: which artery gives the posterior interventricular branch, and which supplies the sinoatrial node, differs between people, so the culprit artery is a best guess. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Missing dual antiplatelet doses in the first 6 weeks after a stent carries what risk of sudden in-stent restenosis?","choices":["About 5%","About 20%","Close to 100%","About 50%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Noncompliance with dual antiplatelet therapy in the first 6 weeks carries a 50% risk of sudden in-stent restenosis, a coin flip. Patients are taught that they have to take these medications. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"When is oxygen given in unstable angina or non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["To every patient on arrival","Only while pain continues","Only after catheterization","Only if SpO2 is under 91%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. MONA (morphine, oxygen, nitroglycerin, aspirin) is outdated: oxygen is given only if the oxygen saturation is below 91%. With normal oxygenation it is not given, because it causes peripheral vasoconstriction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 49, 73"},{"q":"What separates non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction from unstable angina?","choices":["Relief with nitroglycerin","An elevated troponin","Pain at rest","T-wave inversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elevated troponin (standard or high-sensitivity) is what differentiates non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction from unstable angina, whose troponin levels are normal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 47, 86"},{"q":"Which drug is avoided in a right ventricular infarct?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Aspirin","Heparin","Clopidogrel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a right ventricular infarct nitroglycerin is not given: lowering preload removes the Frank-Starling support the failing right ventricle depends on, dropping output and precipitating shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which is the most common form of angina pectoris?","choices":["Vasospastic angina","Microvascular angina","Stable angina","Unstable angina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stable angina, also called stable ischemic heart disease, is the most common form of angina pectoris. It is usually brought on by activity and relieved by rest and nitroglycerin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Microvascular angina was formerly called what?","choices":["Stable ischemic disease","Cardiac syndrome X","Prinzmetal angina","Dressler syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina was formerly called cardiac syndrome X: angina with ST changes, possibly elevated biomarkers, but normal coronary arteries on angiography. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which word appears among the recognized angina descriptors?","choices":["Toothache","Stabbing","Pleuritic","Shooting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angina may be described as squeezing, tightness, pressure, constriction, burning, heartburn, fullness, band-like, or even a toothache, so an odd word does not exclude it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does the T in the HEART score stand for?","choices":["Troponin","Timing of pain","Treadmill result","Temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. HEART (History, Electrocardiogram, Age, Risk factors, Troponin) scores the initial troponin as its last component; the score is one of several acute coronary syndrome risk scores. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Vasospastic angina most often affects which age group?","choices":["Mainly infants","Mostly over 80","Often under 50","Often over 70"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vasospastic angina is relatively uncommon and often affects people under 50, younger than the typical patient with atherosclerotic coronary artery disease. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Once myocardial ischemia begins, within what time does irreversible injury occur?","choices":["5 to 10 minutes","6 to 12 hours","2 to 3 days","40 to 60 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Irreversible myocardial injury occurs in about 40 to 60 minutes of ischemia, which is why the treatment of an acute infarction is built around time: time is muscle. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does coronary angiography show in microvascular angina?","choices":["Left main narrowing","Normal coronary arteries","A single occluded vessel","Provoked coronary spasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina is angina with ST changes, possibly even elevated cardiac biomarkers, but normal coronary arteries on the angiogram. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What is the goal door-to-balloon time for ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Under 10 minutes","Under 30 minutes","Under 4 hours","Under 90 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Time is muscle: the goal door-to-balloon time is under 90 minutes, after an electrocardiogram within 10 minutes of arrival. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Cardiogenic shock after infarction is more common with which infarcts?","choices":["Right-sided infarcts","Silent infarcts","Vasospastic episodes","Left-sided infarcts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic shock is a mechanical complication of myocardial infarction that is more common in left-sided infarcts, in keeping with left ventricular involvement carrying the higher mortality. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 78, 12"},{"q":"How does the pain of unstable angina respond to nitroglycerin?","choices":["Usually relieved, not always","Always completely relieved","Never relieved at all","Made worse by the drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In unstable angina, nitroglycerin usually (but not always) helps. Stable angina is reliably relieved by rest and nitroglycerin, and infarction pain may or may not respond. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 86"}]};
